\f1\fs22 But a deal was a deal that Chanta was determined to collect his part of; Johnny hoped he would be the kind of fool that would estrange Sarah in perpetuity and beyond, and that would be that.\
Johnny met Chanta at the hover pad, in part to save everyone the embarrassment of him trying to enter the club house.\
Chanta walked straight to Johnny. \'93Where\'92s Sarah?\'94\
\'93At our home.\'94\
\'93You mean, Eloise Shakti\'92s home.\'94\
\'93Not under the same roof.\'94\
Chanta snorted.\
\'93The car is this way,\'94 Johnny said, and led off.\
Johnny had only driven over one left-side kerb when Chanta ordered, \'93Pull over.\'94\
Johnny braked and nearly put Chanta\'92s head through the windscreen. Chanta opened the door and swung one leg out.\
\'93What are you doing?\'94 Johnny demanded.\
\'93I\'92ll walk.\'94\
\'93You don\'92t know where it is.\'94\
\'93You drive, I\'92ll follow. I\'92m sure I\'92ll keep up.\'94\
Johnny relaxed his grip on the steering wheel and hung his head. \'93How about you drive?\'94\
Chanta got out of the car and went round the back. Johnny went round the front.\
When they got to the Sublime Point gates, they passed through without challenge. Johnny congratulated himself for making Chanta appear like just his driver rather than a visitor.\
The ground car passed Eloise\'92s light-bending residence that seemed to blend into the background from any angle, past the hover pad that Johnny had forbidden Chanta from landing on, past the auditorium and a long squat office building that seemed to have a train station underneath. They passed into bushland and the road continued in wide sweeping arcs until a small residential stand of townhouses appeared around a bend.\
Chanta remained quiet; Johnny expected more gratitude for the favour he was granting.\
\'93Pull up here,\'94 Johnny directed. \'93It\'92s that one there.\'94\
\'93Are you sure she\'92s home?\'94\
Johnny smiled. \'93I know when she\'92s home, Chanta.\'94\
They both got out. \
\'93If she asks,\'94 Johnny said, \'93this was entirely your idea.\'94\
Chanta crossed the street; Johnny got behind the wheel. \'93Good luck,\'94 he said to the dashboard, started the motor and pulled uneasily away from the kerb.\
Chanta watched the ground car vanish around the bend, then walked up to  the townhouse.\
He knocked. The seconds slowed down.\
Sarah opened the door. For several seconds neither spoke.\
\'93Tony?\'94\
\'93Um, hi. You\'92re looking\'85 pregnant.\'94\
Sarah looked behind him. \'93How did you get in?\'94\
\'93Ground car.\'94\
\'93Where is it now?\'94\
\'93I sent the chauffeur off,\'94 Chanta said, and instantly regretted it.\
\'93Of course,\'94 Sarah said icily.\
It was December, but Chanta felt chilled. \'93Can I come in?\'94\
Sarah paused long enough, then pulled the door back and wandered slowly down the hall. Chanta followed and shut the door behind. Sarah led him to the patio out back.\
\'93Nice place,\'94 Chanta said.\
\'93Nothing like yours.\'94 Sarah stared at him. She folded her arms as far as Little Clinton permitted. \'93What do you want, Tony?\'94\
\'93I can\'92t write.\'94\
Sarah waited.\
\'93I mean, I can write. Technically.\'94 He pulled a pad from his coat and handed it to her.\
Sarah read the page he\'92d opened for her. Then she read it again, apparently to make certain it had not improved while unattended.\
She looked up.\
\'93That\'92s pretty bad.\'94\
Chanta said nothing. He hoped Little Clinton would have started to glow or strike him down with genius by now.\
\'93Are you a rap artist now?\'94 Sarah asked as she flipped through the notebook.\
\'93Not on purpose. It\'92s pretty bad, huh?\'94\
Sarah raised her eyebrows and boggled her eyes. She put the pad down and pushed it back to Chanta. \'93All artists go through dry periods.\'94\
\'93I\'92ve tried everything.\'94\
\'93Have you tried working?\'94\
Chanta looked offended.\
\'93You were under immense stress when you wrote those early pieces. Now you just expect to fart out new lyrics.\'94\
\'93I work.\'94\
\'93You stare at paper and wait for everybody to be right about you.\'94\
Chanta took the pad back. \'93Second album blues, I guess.\'94\
\'93There you go. It\'92s working already.\'94\
\'93It\'92s because I\'92m here.\'94\
\'93No. It\'92s not.\'94\
\'93I need to be near Little Clinton. He\'92s special.\'94\
Sarah looked away. \'93You know, I\'92m tired of hearing that. I\'92m tired of all the adulation, of all the expectation. He\'92s the worst thing that ever happened to me.\'94\
\'93Don\'92t say that.\'94\
Sarah was instantly ashamed of herself. \'93No. You\'92re right. I\'92ve had far worse things happen to me. Enough for five lifetimes.\'94\
Chanta\'92s chest sank, as if he was feeling some of that. \'93I need to be near you.\'94\
Sarah reached out and put her hand on Chanta\'92s. \'93If you want your legendary songwriting ability back, do the work.\'94\
Chanta stared blankly back.\
 Sarah took her hand back. \'93Don\'92t be the legend.\'94\
Chanta closed his eyes. Several seconds passed before they opened. \'93When we lived in that old Elvi mansion, I was happy.\'94\
\'93Well, good for you, because my life was falling apart.\'94\
Chanta seemed genuinely hurt by that, and his pained expression advertised it.\
From somewhere deeper in the house came the unmistakable sound of Kathy dropping something in the kitchen and swearing about it.\
Chanta glanced towards the sound.\
\'93Chanta, you\'92re happy with a cheeseburger. You\'92re happy making a crime scene spotless. You\'92re happy in a seedy bar in the Ghetto. You\'92re happy having a beer with your mates.\'94\
\'93Even Halo?\'94\
\'93Of course not. He was a serial killer and he wanted me dead.\'94\
\'93He\'92s how I found out I was rich.\'94\
Sarah snorted. \'93Possibly the biggest crime of all of them.\'94\
\'93Halo protected me. I\'92d be dead without him.\'94\
Sarah appeared to fall into her belly a little.\
\'93Are you with Johnny now?\'94\
Sarah sat back. \'93What? No! Tony, you\'92re better than\'85\'94 She searched for the words.\
\'93Better than what?\'94 Chanta asked.\
\'93Just\'85 better, that\'92s all.\'94\
Chanta just looked at her.\
Sarah winced and adjusted herself on the bench. \'93I should really get off this thing and onto something with more support.\'94\
\'93I should go.\'94\
They both stood. Sarah walked Chanta to the front door.\
\'93Tony, half the Elvi have shagged the other half because you told them to.\'94\
\'93It\'92s saving them.\'94\
\'93I know.\'94\
\'93You sound angry about that.\'94\
Sarah chewed her bottom lip. \'93I am angry about that.\'94\
Chanta blinked.\
Sarah led Chanta past the doorstep and pulled the door mostly closed behind her. \'93You had sex with how many people?\'94\
\'93I don\'92t know.\'94\
\'93Excellent answer.\'94\
\'93It was an emergency.\'94\
Sarah looked down at Little Clinton, then back at him.\
\'93There\'92s something you don\'92t know.\'94\
Chanta's expression changed.\
\'93I\'92m the source,\'94 Sarah whispered.\
\'93The sauce?\'94 Chanta asked and, despite his flat Ulyssian accent, Sarah knew that he\'92d misunderstood her.\
\'93I\'92m the source of the hump virus. Chilli. TPlus. Whatever Kathy is calling it this week.\'94\
Chanta stared at her; he could hardly hear her.\
\'93I\'92ve had it since I was a child.\'94 Sarah brushed her hair back and placed her other hand on her hip for support. She felt old. \'93You didn\'92t start this, Tony.\'94\
\'93But\'85 Kathy says I\'92ve got it in my DNA.\'94\
\'93So do I,\'94 Sarah whispered flatly.\
\'93Kathy told you that too?\'94\
Sarah recoiled. \'93No. Kathy doesn\'92t know, and she can never know.\'94\
\'93Why are you telling me now?\'94\
\'93Because you cannot be made to bear this, Tony. I\'92m sorry, but there\'92s nothing special about you, at least not in that regard. But the good news, you don\'92t have to carry that; it\'92s not your load, or your responsibility\'97\'94\
\'93Or my superpower.\'94 Chanta looked at her belly again, this time without being told off. \'93I never assaulted you, you know that, right?\'94\
Sarah teared up a little. \'93I know.\'94\
\'93Then how did I get it? How am I the only one\'97apart from you\'97to have it in my DNA?\'94\
\'93My guess? After the Vegas Show, around the same time the Sages impregnated me.\'94 \
\'93The Sages.\'94\
\'93I don\'92t know how but, yes, that\'92s my guess.\'94\
\'93And then I spread it.\'94\
\'93Yes.\'94\
\'93To the Elvi.\'94\
\'93Yes.\'94\
\'93And saved them.\'94\
Sarah sighed. \'93You still turned an epidemiological emergency into the world's most badly supervised super-orgy.\'94\
\'93Can you say that again? I want to get it right for my C.V.\'94\
Sarah choked back a laugh. \'93Do you have a KinkedIn profile, Tony?\'94\
\'93Sure. All the best cleaners do.\'94\
Sarah looked at him for a long moment. \'93You did save them.\'94\
Chanta looked down at his shoes.\
\'93You\'92re such a boofhead,\'94 Sarah proclaimed, but Chanta did not see the affection in her eyes or her smile.\
 \'93I\'92ve been a rich boofhead,\'94 Chanta agreed.\
Sarah considered this. \'93You were a poor boofhead first.\'94\
\'93I know.\'94\
\'93The rich one was worse.\'94\
\'93I know.\'94\
The door remained mostly closed. Chanta looked out to the street and the surrounding bush. \'93I\'92ve got something to show you,\'94 he said.\
Sarah became immediately suspicious.\
\'93I think you'll like it.\'94 He started undoing his belt.\
\'93Tony.\'94\
He unfastened his trousers.\
\'93Tony, stop.\'94\
\'93I was never proud of this before I met you.\'94\
\'93If you take those down out here\'97\'94\
Chanta pulled the waistband down.\
Pink fairies danced across his boxer shorts.\
Sarah stared down into Chanta\'92s pants and started laughing. She wondered what anyone spying would make of that. The fairies had wings, wands and an apparently thriving mushroom-based economy underneath them.\
Her mouth opened. \'93Mr Fairy Boxers.\'94\
Chanta smiled.\
\'93You know, I bought a pack of those for you, months ago. I never got to give them to you.\'94\
Sarah leaned in. \'93Give me time, Tony. Let\'92s see what sort of mother I make first, okay?\'94\
Chanta nodded, steeling himself for the wait.\
\'93And Little Clinton is not your crutch, okay?\'94\
\'93Get back to cleaning.\'94\
Chanta's smile faltered. \'93What?\'94\
\'93You heard me. Mr Fairy Boxers had a job.\'94\
\'93I\'92m the Great Teacher.\'94\
\'93I need no schooling, Mr Fairy Boxers.\'94\
\'93I have people who clean for me now.\'94\
Sarah shrugged. \'93Sure. You gotta keep that obscene penthouse scrubbed. But you can clean the things that make you happy.\'94\
\'93Murder scenes.\'94 Chanta grinned.\
\'93I\'92d keep that to yourself.\'94\
\'93I will. So can I come hang around you and Little Clinton from time to time?\'94\
Sarah exhaled. \'93Do the work. Don\'92t be the legend.\'94\
Chanta slumped. \'93Okay.\'94\
\'93And do your pants up.\'94\
